2. Four-Step Rule for Section 300 Clause Thirdly: To bring an offence under Section 300 Thirdly ('if it is done with the intention of causing bodily injury to any person and the bodily injury intended to be inflicted is sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death'), the prosecution must prove four elements:
First, it must establish, quite objectively, that a bodily injury is present;
Second, the nature of the injury must be proved. These are purely objective investigations;
Third, it must be proved that there was an intention to inflict that particular bodily injury, that is to say, that it was not accidental or unintentional, or that some other kind of injury was intended;
Fourth, it must be proved that the injury of the type just described made up of the three elements set out above was sufficient to cause death in the ordinary course of nature.
